  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Interior obligated $4.3B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department awarded 19,483 contracts so far, with an average award size of $221K, and obligations fell 34.2% year over year. The Department's current-year total is partial; historical full-year obligations show a peak at $8.1B in FY24, followed by $6.6B in FY25 and the FY26 year-to-date total of $4.3B. Construction and IT services dominate the top categories: Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction accounted for $589M, Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounted for $401M, and Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $348M. The Department directed sizable work to a mix of large systems integrators and regional construction firms: GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. received $136M, AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC received $132M, and RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. received $129M. The data show Interior focusing on infrastructure and information technology while award counts and total obligations have declined year over year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Department of the Interior contract spending changed over recent years?",
        "body": "The Department of the Interior obligated $4.3B in FY26 to date, after $6.6B in FY25 and $8.1B in FY24. The Department experienced a year-over-year decline, with obligations down 34.2%. The Department awarded 19,483 contracts in FY26 to date, with an average award size of $221K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are Interior's top contract categories and who are the leading vendors?",
        "body": "Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction led categories with $589M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $401M and Computer Systems Design Services at $348M. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. received $136M, AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC received $132M, and RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. received $129M. The top categories mix construction and IT services, showing procurement across infrastructure and technology."
      }
